1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,808
You wanted to be a Viking.

2
00:00:01,808 --> 00:00:04,566
A ship, a sword, a terrifying reputation.

3
00:00:04,566 --> 00:00:08,639
Instead, it is barely light, your shoes
are wet, and a sheep

4
00:00:08,639 --> 00:00:10,933
has escaped through the fence you repaired
yesterday.

5
00:00:11,511 --> 00:00:14,947
Your partner points at the broken fence,
then the empty wood basket.

6
00:00:15,685 --> 00:00:18,374
Your first conquest of the morning will be
firewood.

7
00:00:18,374 --> 00:00:22,518
Imagine you are Eirik, a free farmer on
Norway's western coast

8
00:00:22,518 --> 00:00:23,915
around the year nine hundred and fifty.

9
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:28,555
This is an imagined household, built
around what archaeology tells us

10
00:00:28,555 --> 00:00:32,925
about Viking Age life. You have land to
work, relatives to

11
00:00:32,925 --> 00:00:35,550
rely on, and the opportunity to join a
voyage.

12
00:00:36,304 --> 00:00:39,161
That does not make your life comfortable.

13
00:00:39,161 --> 00:00:43,643
And one distinction matters. Norse society
was not an entire

14
00:00:43,643 --> 00:00:47,994
population of full-time raiders. Farming,
fishing, crafts,

15
00:00:48,010 --> 00:00:51,843
and trade supported it. Going on a Viking
expedition was something

16
00:00:51,923 --> 00:00:55,727
some people did. You might spend a season
overseas and the next

17
00:00:55,775 --> 00:01:00,163
repairing a roof. The rest involves
keeping your household alive until

18
00:01:00,163 --> 00:01:03,864
the weather improves. The National Museum
of Denmark describes the

19
00:01:03,864 --> 00:01:08,271
typical Viking Age household as farming
and raising animals, with

20
00:01:08,271 --> 00:01:10,130
food planned around the seasons.

21
00:01:10,885 --> 00:01:14,786
Your real enemy has an excellent annual
schedule: winter.

22
00:01:15,381 --> 00:01:18,351
Before we give you the ship, therefore,
you get the farm.

23
00:01:18,752 --> 00:01:22,622
Because if you cannot feed yourself here,
crossing the North Sea will

24
00:01:23,122 --> 00:01:26,251
not magically improve your qualifications
as a living person.

25
00:01:27,102 --> 00:01:30,731
You wake inside a timber house, with
sleeping spaces along the

26
00:01:31,231 --> 00:01:33,513
sides and a hearth at its heart.

27
00:01:33,513 --> 00:01:35,589
Fire gives warmth and light.

28
00:01:35,589 --> 00:01:39,000
Smoke is the less charming member of the
household.

29
00:01:39,626 --> 00:01:43,881
There is no neat little chimney quietly
removing every inconvenience.

30
00:01:44,602 --> 00:01:48,512
Roof openings and ventilation help, but
the hearth still shapes

31
00:01:49,012 --> 00:01:52,727
your indoor air. You get close for warmth,
then move away

32
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:57,518
coughing. Breakfast means something the
household actually has:

33
00:01:57,518 --> 00:02:01,136
perhaps grain porridge, bread, or
preserved fish.

34
00:02:01,136 --> 00:02:05,194
There can be dairy, meat, vegetables, and
berries too.

35
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:09,329
Viking food was varied; availability was
the catch.

36
00:02:10,163 --> 00:02:12,316
An impressive feast is possible.

37
00:02:12,316 --> 00:02:16,208
It is simply not guaranteed every time you
sit down.

38
00:02:16,208 --> 00:02:20,487
Somebody grew, caught, processed, or
exchanged everything in

39
00:02:20,487 --> 00:02:24,807
that bowl. Your partner, Aslaug, checks
the stored

40
00:02:24,807 --> 00:02:27,757
grain while you pull on a wool tunic.

41
00:02:27,757 --> 00:02:31,812
Clothing is valuable work. Spinning,
weaving,

42
00:02:31,812 --> 00:02:36,253
cutting, and repairing take time that your
fantasy outfit never

43
00:02:36,253 --> 00:02:40,220
mentioned. Surviving clothing evidence
shows wool and linen,

44
00:02:40,493 --> 00:02:42,963
with differences in wealth and display.

45
00:02:42,963 --> 00:02:47,339
Yours is patched. Replacing the wardrobe
whenever a sleeve tears

46
00:02:47,339 --> 00:02:49,960
is not a reasonable household policy.

47
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:54,148
You wrap yourself against the cold, fasten
your cloak, and discover

48
00:02:54,148 --> 00:02:58,613
another problem. Leather shoes protect
your feet, but a muddy yard

49
00:02:58,613 --> 00:03:02,994
remains a muddy yard. You are already
carrying it indoors.

50
00:03:02,994 --> 00:03:07,012
Outside, your animals need attention
before your ambitions do.

51
00:03:07,526 --> 00:03:10,652
A cow that produces milk is also a cow
that eats.

52
00:03:11,293 --> 00:03:15,036
Keeping livestock means arranging food for
creatures that have never

53
00:03:15,036 --> 00:03:19,214
contributed silver. You turn the soil and
work the small fields.

54
00:03:19,967 --> 00:03:24,138
In this household, grain, grazing, and
fishing all matter.

55
00:03:24,138 --> 00:03:28,535
The balance varies across Scandinavia;
this coastal farm is

56
00:03:28,535 --> 00:03:32,031
not every Norse person's identical
experience.

57
00:03:32,031 --> 00:03:34,129
The growing season is a deadline.

58
00:03:34,804 --> 00:03:37,904
Rain can help the crops, then ruin your
plans.

59
00:03:38,643 --> 00:03:41,875
You cannot ask the weather to reconsider
because your future voyage

60
00:03:41,875 --> 00:03:44,891
sounds important. Hay must be cut and
stored.

61
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:49,420
During the colder months, animals cannot
live on your inspirational

62
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:53,944
speech. Every useful bundle you carry now
is a small argument

63
00:03:53,944 --> 00:03:58,127
against disaster later. Aslaug carries out
work of her own,

64
00:03:58,127 --> 00:04:02,228
managing supplies and textiles, alongside
the household's other

65
00:04:02,228 --> 00:04:06,483
tasks. Women contributed substantial labor
and responsibility.

66
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:10,465
That does not mean Viking society offered
modern equality.

67
00:04:10,465 --> 00:04:14,731
Everyone's place matters. The National
Museum describes free people,

68
00:04:15,244 --> 00:04:17,696
powerful elites, and enslaved people.

69
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:21,003
Those at the bottom could be bought and
sold.

70
00:04:21,003 --> 00:04:25,002
Your freedom is an advantage, not the
universal starting point.

71
00:04:25,002 --> 00:04:29,431
There is a darker foundation beneath some
famous voyages: people were

72
00:04:29,431 --> 00:04:33,710
captured and traded. That is not
adventurous treasure hunting.

73
00:04:34,303 --> 00:04:38,230
For those taken, somebody else's profit
meant the destruction of

74
00:04:38,730 --> 00:04:42,317
their own freedom. You, meanwhile, have a
more ordinary problem.

75
00:04:42,991 --> 00:04:47,426
A tool needs repair. Ironwork takes skill
and resources, and

76
00:04:47,426 --> 00:04:49,576
the smith expects something in return.

77
00:04:49,576 --> 00:04:52,558
Your axe cannot be maintained by glaring
at it.

78
00:04:52,558 --> 00:04:55,669
You trade what you can spare, then bring
the repaired tool home.

79
00:04:55,669 --> 00:04:59,509
It is a better investment than owning a
shiny weapon while your

80
00:04:59,509 --> 00:05:03,540
fence collapses. Then Bjorn arrives with
news of a summer voyage.

81
00:05:03,540 --> 00:05:07,004
He has access to a boat and a crew, and
enough standing

82
00:05:07,004 --> 00:05:08,933
to put the expedition together.

83
00:05:08,933 --> 00:05:12,233
You have enthusiasm and extremely modest
savings.

84
00:05:12,233 --> 00:05:16,662
He talks about opportunities overseas:
trade, payment,

85
00:05:16,662 --> 00:05:20,023
perhaps plunder if circumstances favor it.

86
00:05:20,023 --> 00:05:24,219
Those activities existed together in the
wider Viking world.

87
00:05:24,219 --> 00:05:27,340
A man carrying goods might also be
carrying weapons.

88
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:30,747
Your decision is not simply courage versus
cowardice.

89
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:33,856
If you leave, someone must do your work
here.

90
00:05:34,689 --> 00:05:36,851
Aslaug looks at the remaining tasks.

91
00:05:36,851 --> 00:05:40,370
Suddenly the voyage has an extra
passenger: guilt.

92
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:44,516
You discuss household needs, crew
expectations, and what you can

93
00:05:44,516 --> 00:05:47,543
contribute. This bargain is imagined.

94
00:05:47,543 --> 00:05:51,102
The underlying problem is real: boats,

95
00:05:51,102 --> 00:05:55,361
equipment, food, and labor require
resources.

96
00:05:55,361 --> 00:05:59,502
At the local assembly, free men gather to
discuss matters and

97
00:05:59,502 --> 00:06:03,818
disputes. Norse law was not just everyone
swinging an axe until

98
00:06:03,818 --> 00:06:07,922
agreement happened. Assemblies provided a
place for

99
00:06:07,922 --> 00:06:12,287
negotiation and judgment. Your neighbor
raises a boundary complaint.

100
00:06:12,897 --> 00:06:14,905
You think his claim is ridiculous.

101
00:06:14,905 --> 00:06:17,601
He thinks your confidence is ridiculous.

102
00:06:17,601 --> 00:06:21,749
Other people listen, because land and
reputation matter.

103
00:06:21,749 --> 00:06:25,521
You cannot simply announce that you are
the main character.

104
00:06:25,521 --> 00:06:29,643
Family connections and influential support
affect your position.

105
00:06:29,643 --> 00:06:33,299
A free farmer has a voice; that does not
make him equal

106
00:06:33,299 --> 00:06:37,645
to the wealthiest man. The issue is left
for further discussion, and

107
00:06:37,645 --> 00:06:42,139
you return to preparations. You would
prefer to solve one crisis

108
00:06:42,139 --> 00:06:46,026
at a time. Viking Age life has rejected
that feature request.

109
00:06:46,026 --> 00:06:49,308
Down at the landing, the boat needs work.

110
00:06:49,308 --> 00:06:53,077
Planks, fastenings, and seams must hold.

111
00:06:54,054 --> 00:06:56,748
Ships were remarkable achievements of
craftsmanship.

112
00:06:57,597 --> 00:07:02,018
Remarkable does not mean they maintained
themselves while the crew

113
00:07:02,018 --> 00:07:06,457
practiced looking dangerous. You check an
oar and help with the sail.

114
00:07:06,457 --> 00:07:10,586
A large textile sail represents
considerable labor.

115
00:07:10,586 --> 00:07:14,508
Aslaug's world of wool and careful hands
has more to do with

116
00:07:14,508 --> 00:07:16,774
your expedition than the songs suggest.

117
00:07:17,624 --> 00:07:21,890
Food and water must come aboard, along
with goods, tools, and

118
00:07:21,890 --> 00:07:25,016
personal belongings. Space is limited.

119
00:07:25,016 --> 00:07:29,258
You quickly learn the difference between
packing for adventure and

120
00:07:29,258 --> 00:07:31,712
packing for a boat that must actually
float.

121
00:07:31,712 --> 00:07:35,763
Now the weapons. You have a spear and a
round shield.

122
00:07:35,763 --> 00:07:38,452
Bjorn's sword is much more impressive.

123
00:07:39,207 --> 00:07:43,411
The National Museum notes that swords were
expensive, while axes and

124
00:07:43,411 --> 00:07:47,809
spears were more accessible. Your lack of
a sword does

125
00:07:47,809 --> 00:07:50,123
not disqualify you from being Norse.

126
00:07:50,123 --> 00:07:54,167
It mainly disqualifies you from looking
exactly like an expensive

127
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:58,359
television character. Wealth has its own
costume department.

128
00:07:58,359 --> 00:07:59,883
And the helmet with horns?

129
00:08:00,669 --> 00:08:02,104
Remove it from the picture.

130
00:08:02,104 --> 00:08:06,279
The museum's helmet evidence does not
support that familiar battle

131
00:08:06,279 --> 00:08:10,449
costume. You are traveling bareheaded,
with a hood available when the

132
00:08:10,449 --> 00:08:13,628
weather turns. Finally, you push off.

133
00:08:13,628 --> 00:08:18,057
Aslaug remains at the landing while your
world narrows to

134
00:08:18,057 --> 00:08:21,334
wood, rope, water, and other men's elbows.

135
00:08:21,334 --> 00:08:25,248
Then the wind changes. Oars become work
instead of

136
00:08:25,248 --> 00:08:28,920
decoration. You pull, recover, and pull
again.

137
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:32,544
The man beside you is doing the same, so
complaining does not

138
00:08:32,544 --> 00:08:36,838
make you particularly interesting. When
the sail takes over, you still

139
00:08:36,838 --> 00:08:41,037
have jobs. Handling the vessel, watching
conditions, managing

140
00:08:41,037 --> 00:08:43,993
equipment, and keeping things in order
demand attention.

141
00:08:44,973 --> 00:08:48,122
Nobody has employed you as a full-time
horizon admirer.

142
00:08:48,829 --> 00:08:50,425
The ship is open to the weather.

143
00:08:50,425 --> 00:08:52,215
Your blanket is not a private cabin.

144
00:08:52,856 --> 00:08:56,030
Rain lands on your clothes, your
belongings, and your mood.

145
00:08:56,623 --> 00:08:59,958
Dry comfort becomes something you remember
with surprising affection.

146
00:09:00,615 --> 00:09:04,482
You follow coasts when practical, stop
where conditions allow, and

147
00:09:04,482 --> 00:09:08,740
depend on experienced navigation. Crossing
open water remains

148
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:12,943
serious work. Our imagined crew is heading
toward England, not

149
00:09:12,943 --> 00:09:15,650
visiting three continents before supper.

150
00:09:15,650 --> 00:09:19,446
On a rougher stretch, water comes aboard
and you help bail.

151
00:09:19,446 --> 00:09:21,939
The sea has no opinion about your courage.

152
00:09:22,773 --> 00:09:26,970
It only requires the boat to remain
seaworthy, whether you feel heroic

153
00:09:26,970 --> 00:09:30,441
or not. At night, you try to rest in
cramped

154
00:09:30,941 --> 00:09:35,328
conditions. Someone snores. Someone shifts
an elbow into your

155
00:09:35,328 --> 00:09:39,550
ribs. You have crossed a vast body of
water to discover that

156
00:09:39,550 --> 00:09:42,969
roommates are universal. You eat from the
supplies and drink

157
00:09:42,969 --> 00:09:46,081
carefully. Every extra day draws on what
you brought.

158
00:09:46,803 --> 00:09:50,563
Weather delays can turn patience into a
practical problem, even

159
00:09:50,563 --> 00:09:52,704
without a dramatic storm swallowing the
ship.

160
00:09:53,537 --> 00:09:55,908
Eventually you approach the English coast.

161
00:09:55,908 --> 00:09:59,695
Here, too, people know about Scandinavian
travelers and settlements.

162
00:10:00,288 --> 00:10:04,659
You are entering an inhabited, politically
complicated world, not an

163
00:10:04,659 --> 00:10:07,118
empty map labeled free treasure.

164
00:10:07,118 --> 00:10:11,147
Bjorn listens to local information before
deciding where to go.

165
00:10:11,147 --> 00:10:13,942
A poorly defended target might offer
wealth.

166
00:10:13,942 --> 00:10:17,476
It might also be less poorly defended than
the person describing it

167
00:10:17,476 --> 00:10:18,962
would like you to believe.

168
00:10:18,962 --> 00:10:22,727
You land quietly away from the trading
place and carry your shield

169
00:10:22,727 --> 00:10:26,922
inland with the crew. This is the portion
people imagine first.

170
00:10:26,922 --> 00:10:30,820
Your stomach has suddenly remembered the
portion where you could die.

171
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:33,417
Across a small open space, armed men
appear.

172
00:10:33,417 --> 00:10:36,707
They are not waiting politely to become an
anecdote.

173
00:10:36,707 --> 00:10:40,625
They have homes, allies, and weapons, and
very little enthusiasm for

174
00:10:40,625 --> 00:10:44,352
your proposed business model. Bjorn
considers the ground and the

175
00:10:44,352 --> 00:10:47,892
opposition. A retreat can preserve people
and equipment.

176
00:10:47,892 --> 00:10:51,619
This crew chooses to withdraw rather than
force an attack.

177
00:10:51,619 --> 00:10:55,572
There is no guaranteed victory hidden in
your nationality.

178
00:10:55,572 --> 00:10:57,467
Back at the boat, an argument starts.

179
00:10:58,126 --> 00:11:00,308
One man calls the decision cautious.

180
00:11:00,308 --> 00:11:04,577
Another calls it humiliating. You call it
excellent while privately

181
00:11:04,577 --> 00:11:08,050
checking that all your important body
parts are still present.

182
00:11:08,050 --> 00:11:11,987
That encounter is fictional, but the
choice illustrates

183
00:11:11,987 --> 00:11:16,210
something basic: overseas profit depended
on conditions.

184
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:19,261
Not every expedition achieved a dramatic
conquest.

185
00:11:20,032 --> 00:11:23,870
Failed opportunities still consumed food,
time, and effort.

186
00:11:24,705 --> 00:11:28,311
The crew changes its plan and approaches a
place where exchange is

187
00:11:28,311 --> 00:11:32,075
possible. Weapons stay present, but the
goods come out.

188
00:11:32,813 --> 00:11:36,081
You are now hoping a merchant likes your
cargo more than your

189
00:11:36,081 --> 00:11:40,426
expression. The harbor has timber
buildings, working people, and

190
00:11:40,426 --> 00:11:44,586
ordinary noise. Traders need information,
contacts, and

191
00:11:44,586 --> 00:11:48,847
something others want. Being able to
frighten a stranger is

192
00:11:48,847 --> 00:11:51,827
not the same as securing a good bargain.

193
00:11:51,827 --> 00:11:55,518
You inspect cloth while the merchant
inspects your goods.

194
00:11:55,518 --> 00:11:59,665
There are negotiations, misunderstandings,
and the uncomfortable

195
00:11:59,665 --> 00:12:03,519
possibility that the other person
understands the transaction

196
00:12:03,519 --> 00:12:05,737
considerably better than you do.

197
00:12:05,737 --> 00:12:08,655
Silver is useful, but its form varies.

198
00:12:09,184 --> 00:12:12,732
Viking Age trade involved coins and silver
valued by weight,

199
00:12:13,182 --> 00:12:16,440
including cut pieces. You watch a balance
carefully.

200
00:12:17,115 --> 00:12:19,924
Confidence is not a substitute for the
correct weight.

201
00:12:20,711 --> 00:12:25,008
The merchant adds a weight; Bjorn answers;
the bargaining

202
00:12:25,008 --> 00:12:29,211
continues. Your imagined sale brings some
silver and

203
00:12:29,211 --> 00:12:33,510
supplies. It does not finance a
magnificent hall, a second

204
00:12:33,510 --> 00:12:36,379
ship, and retirement before your next
birthday.

205
00:12:37,325 --> 00:12:40,709
Nearby, another traveler shows an object
from far away.

206
00:12:41,334 --> 00:12:44,108
Viking networks reached extraordinary
distances.

207
00:12:44,766 --> 00:12:48,935
That does not mean every farmer personally
sailed the entire network.

208
00:12:49,592 --> 00:12:52,092
Goods could travel through many hands.

209
00:12:52,092 --> 00:12:55,374
You buy a small useful item to bring home.

210
00:12:55,374 --> 00:12:59,767
Not a mountain of jeweled treasure:
something Aslaug can use

211
00:12:59,767 --> 00:13:03,550
and recognize as evidence that your
absence produced more than

212
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:07,818
interesting stories. Before leaving, the
crew repairs worn

213
00:13:07,818 --> 00:13:12,233
gear. Staying ashore costs time, but
ignoring damage could

214
00:13:12,233 --> 00:13:16,239
cost much more. Even adventure requires
maintenance appointments.

215
00:13:16,832 --> 00:13:19,059
Bjorn gathers everyone for the return.

216
00:13:19,059 --> 00:13:23,262
You check the water containers, secure the
cargo, and look back at

217
00:13:23,262 --> 00:13:26,136
the harbor. You have earned something.

218
00:13:26,136 --> 00:13:29,662
Now you must keep it through another
stretch of sea.

219
00:13:29,662 --> 00:13:33,149
Home is not one uninterrupted victory lap
away.

220
00:13:33,149 --> 00:13:35,483
There are winds to judge and stops to
make.

221
00:13:36,124 --> 00:13:40,518
You are tired of being damp, which
unfortunately is not a useful

222
00:13:40,518 --> 00:13:43,394
navigation method. A cloak shifts in the
wind and you pull it

223
00:13:43,394 --> 00:13:47,755
close. Sometimes discomfort is simply
hours of being miserable while

224
00:13:47,755 --> 00:13:51,859
remaining very much alive. You share work
and supplies with the crew.

225
00:13:52,566 --> 00:13:56,132
The voyage depends on cooperation,
including with people you would

226
00:13:56,132 --> 00:13:58,558
never voluntarily invite to dinner.

227
00:13:58,558 --> 00:14:01,513
Your survival has acquired a group
project.

228
00:14:01,513 --> 00:14:05,775
When Norway's coast comes into view,
familiar land feels different.

229
00:14:05,775 --> 00:14:09,638
The fields did not suspend themselves
during your adventure.

230
00:14:09,638 --> 00:14:13,973
Neither did the animals, the household, or
the neighbor's deeply

231
00:14:13,973 --> 00:14:18,455
committed boundary complaint. At the
landing, Aslaug greets you,

232
00:14:18,455 --> 00:14:20,250
then looks at what you brought.

233
00:14:20,845 --> 00:14:22,547
You produce the useful gift.

234
00:14:22,547 --> 00:14:24,426
She produces a list of repairs.

235
00:14:25,101 --> 00:14:28,281
Both of you have been working; only one
has sea stories.

236
00:14:29,036 --> 00:14:31,895
The crew divides the proceeds according to
its arrangement.

237
00:14:32,570 --> 00:14:35,509
We cannot supply a universal Viking pay
table.

238
00:14:36,167 --> 00:14:39,727
Shares and relationships differed. In this
household's

239
00:14:40,227 --> 00:14:43,415
imagined reckoning, your reward is welcome
but modest.

240
00:14:44,024 --> 00:14:47,360
You set silver beside the expenses waiting
at home.

241
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:51,587
The tool, the roof, the supplies, the

242
00:14:51,587 --> 00:14:55,649
help you relied upon. Your triumphant
return begins looking

243
00:14:55,649 --> 00:14:59,002
suspiciously like money arriving just
before the bills.

244
00:14:59,676 --> 00:15:02,139
Then the harvest demands your attention.

245
00:15:02,139 --> 00:15:06,317
You work while the weather allows, gather
what you can,

246
00:15:06,317 --> 00:15:08,854
and protect what must last.

247
00:15:08,854 --> 00:15:12,673
There is a deadline even if nobody writes
it on a

248
00:15:12,673 --> 00:15:16,741
calendar. Food is dried, smoked, or
otherwise preserved as

249
00:15:16,741 --> 00:15:20,477
appropriate. Storage is part of survival,
not merely

250
00:15:20,477 --> 00:15:24,956
housekeeping. A meal you cannot keep until
later cannot help you

251
00:15:24,956 --> 00:15:27,629
through the part of the year with less
food.

252
00:15:27,629 --> 00:15:31,190
Aslaug checks containers while you stack
firewood.

253
00:15:31,687 --> 00:15:34,014
The household prepares for shorter days.

254
00:15:34,014 --> 00:15:35,603
A good store is reassuring.

255
00:15:36,197 --> 00:15:39,808
It is also a reminder that nearly
everything here can be used

256
00:15:40,308 --> 00:15:43,180
up. Your animals must be fed and
sheltered.

257
00:15:43,180 --> 00:15:47,222
Keeping them alive protects future milk,
breeding, and work.

258
00:15:48,217 --> 00:15:52,311
Winter is not just you feeling cold; it is
the whole

259
00:15:52,311 --> 00:15:54,379
household consuming its earlier decisions.

260
00:15:55,374 --> 00:15:58,200
Indoors, a tear in your tunic gets
repaired.

261
00:15:58,971 --> 00:16:01,926
Outside, wind finds another weak spot in
the house.

262
00:16:02,744 --> 00:16:06,459
The season has a talent for testing every
job you vaguely remember

263
00:16:06,459 --> 00:16:10,676
meaning to finish. You mend the roof
before the problem grows.

264
00:16:11,592 --> 00:16:15,156
Keeping rain out of the sleeping space
will improve your life more

265
00:16:15,204 --> 00:16:19,219
than a beautiful nickname. A sore tooth
makes eating unpleasant.

266
00:16:20,102 --> 00:16:23,736
Archaeological remains show that Viking
Age people faced dental

267
00:16:23,736 --> 00:16:28,186
problems and injuries. You have people who
may help, but you do

268
00:16:28,186 --> 00:16:30,466
not have modern medicine waiting nearby.

269
00:16:31,414 --> 00:16:35,485
You should not picture every Norse person
filthy, sick, and

270
00:16:35,485 --> 00:16:39,938
indifferent to appearance. Combs and other
grooming objects

271
00:16:39,938 --> 00:16:44,348
survive. Caring about your hair, however,
does not give

272
00:16:44,348 --> 00:16:47,553
you antibiotics or emergency dentistry.

273
00:16:47,553 --> 00:16:50,439
You sit near the hearth while Aslaug works
wool.

274
00:16:51,275 --> 00:16:55,662
The evening offers conversation, repairs,
and perhaps a

275
00:16:55,662 --> 00:17:00,057
game. Life has pleasures. A difficult
existence is

276
00:17:00,057 --> 00:17:03,133
not necessarily an existence without
affection or laughter.

277
00:17:04,049 --> 00:17:06,164
Then your neighbor returns to the dispute.

278
00:17:06,164 --> 00:17:08,082
He thinks you moved a boundary marker.

279
00:17:08,789 --> 00:17:11,488
You think he has developed a remarkably
flexible memory.

280
00:17:12,163 --> 00:17:15,618
This time you bring someone who remembers
the earlier arrangement.

281
00:17:16,325 --> 00:17:19,062
The matter reaches the local assembly
again.

282
00:17:19,062 --> 00:17:22,655
You speak, the neighbor speaks, and other
men weigh the claims.

283
00:17:23,314 --> 00:17:27,611
Evidence, witnesses, negotiation, and
standing matter more

284
00:17:27,611 --> 00:17:29,960
here than a dramatic battle pose.

285
00:17:29,960 --> 00:17:33,113
A compromise settles this imagined
dispute.

286
00:17:33,113 --> 00:17:37,553
You dislike giving ground, but an
escalating feud would cost far

287
00:17:37,553 --> 00:17:41,835
more. The ability to swing a weapon is
less useful than keeping

288
00:17:41,835 --> 00:17:46,043
tomorrow reasonably predictable. Bjorn's
influence helped

289
00:17:46,059 --> 00:17:50,046
bring people together, and you have
obligations within that network.

290
00:17:50,046 --> 00:17:53,837
Leaders could cultivate followers through
wealth and gifts.

291
00:17:53,837 --> 00:17:58,182
A generous patron is still someone whose
wishes can matter a

292
00:17:58,182 --> 00:18:02,571
great deal. You attend a gathering and
listen while better-connected

293
00:18:02,571 --> 00:18:06,545
men speak. Power in this world is uneven.

294
00:18:06,545 --> 00:18:10,789
Being free gives you possibilities that an
enslaved person lacks,

295
00:18:11,271 --> 00:18:14,315
but it does not make every door yours.

296
00:18:14,315 --> 00:18:17,623
Back home, you look at the remaining
grain.

297
00:18:17,623 --> 00:18:19,995
You are neither ruined nor rich.

298
00:18:19,995 --> 00:18:24,197
You survived a voyage, avoided a fight,
made some

299
00:18:24,197 --> 00:18:27,862
trades, and returned to work that never
actually ended.

300
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:31,633
That is the disappointment hidden inside
the Viking fantasy.

301
00:18:32,707 --> 00:18:35,406
Adventure might change your fortunes.

302
00:18:35,406 --> 00:18:39,765
It does not erase seasons,
responsibilities, weather, or

303
00:18:39,765 --> 00:18:42,077
people with more power than you.

304
00:18:42,077 --> 00:18:46,425
The spectacular stories deserve attention,
but so do the

305
00:18:46,425 --> 00:18:50,640
hands that made sails, raised animals,
prepared food,

306
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:55,098
and kept houses standing. Without those
people, the famous

307
00:18:55,098 --> 00:18:56,783
ships would have gone nowhere.

308
00:18:57,553 --> 00:18:59,749
You finally lie down beside the hearth.

309
00:18:59,749 --> 00:19:03,467
Tomorrow brings another fence, another
chore, another

310
00:19:03,467 --> 00:19:07,374
calculation. Your terrifying reputation
will have to wait until you

311
00:19:07,374 --> 00:19:09,620
have finished keeping the household alive.

312
00:19:10,311 --> 00:19:12,525
So why was being a Viking worse than you
think?

313
00:19:13,199 --> 00:19:17,147
Because the sword was optional, the glory
was uncertain, and the work

314
00:19:17,195 --> 00:19:20,806
was unavoidable. Especially when that
sheep has escaped

315
00:19:21,306 --> 00:19:25,443
again. This is Viking Chapters: the lives
behind the legends.
